#!/usr/bin/env perl
# coxph()'s counting-process data, strata, case weights, offsets, clusters and
# formula interface, cross-validated against survival::coxph().
#
# PROVENANCE
#
# %DATA and %EXPECT are frozen output of t/coxph_extended.R.R under R 4.6.1
# with survival 3.8-12 (re-run with `Rscript t/coxph_extended.R.R`; it also
# writes t/bladder.csv, t/bladder2.csv and t/phreg_*.csv).  The corpora and
# models are survival's own tests and statsmodels':
#
#   testw*     survival tests/doweight.R: case weights, and the same data done
#              by replication, which must give the same fit; with a robust
#              variance, and with the replicates clustered.
#   test1*     survival tests/counting.R: the simplest test data set and the
#              same subjects split into (start, stop] intervals.
#   bladder*   survival tests/bladder.R: Wei, Lin and Weissfeld's marginal
#              model (strata by recurrence, covariate-by-stratum
#              interactions, clustered by patient), Andersen and Gill's
#              counting-process model clustered by patient, and Prentice's
#              conditional models on the recurrence subsets.
#   phreg_*    statsmodels 0.14.6 duration/tests/test_phreg.py: its
#              survival_data_<n>_<p>.csv corpora (copied to t/phreg_*.csv),
#              fitted plain, with entry times, with strata
#              (kron(0:4, rep(1, n/5))) and with both, each by Breslow and by
#              Efron.  %STATSMODELS holds the same fits' coefficients and
#              standard errors as that suite's results/survival_r_results.py
#              pins them (R, seven figures).
#
# TOLERANCE
#
# 1e-9 relative on coefficients, standard errors (model-based, robust and
# naive), log-likelihoods and the score, Wald and robust score statistics.
# The accumulation here follows agfit4.c's order of operations and the Newton
# iteration stops on survival's own test, so the two agree to rounding; the
# observed worst is printed at the end, with where it was: 2.5e-14 on this
# build.  %STATSMODELS is printed to seven significant figures, so it is held
# to half a unit in the seventh figure, the most its rounding can be off by;
# the worst observed was 0.999 of it, which is simply rounding at work.

